# Kashika reference

Kashika draws technical diagrams from `.kash` files. There is no auto-layout: you place every box and route every line, and `kashika check` reports what is wrong with the geometry. This page is the core guide; the topics at the end go deeper, and `kashika ref <code>` explains one diagnostic (`kashika ref through`).

## The loop

1. Sketch the layout as a grid: rows and columns with 60–80px gaps, and free lanes for the lines.
2. Write the file. Leave `size` off boxes so they fit their text (`kashika ref sizing`).
3. `kashika check file.kash`. Fix every error first, starting with the first, then every warning. The `help` lines name the fix: did-you-mean names, required sizes, clear lanes (`free`), `label@t` positions, exact anchors.
4. When a fix needs coordinates, `kashika inspect file.kash <ids> --ports` prints absolute boxes, anchors and line vertices, `--corridors x1,y1,x2,y2` lists free lanes, and `kashika route file.kash a.right b.left` suggests ready-to-paste lines (`kashika ref cli`).
5. When the check is clean, `kashika render file.kash -o file.png` and look at the picture. A clean check is not a good diagram.

## Rules people get wrong

1. **y grows down.** `(0,0)` is the top-left corner of the canvas, and `v -40` moves up.
2. **Literal coordinates are in the frame of the enclosing group.** `at (10,20)` inside `group vpc at (100,100) ...` lands at canvas `(110,120)`. Anchors, `h`/`v` moves and `+(dx,dy)` offsets are the same in every frame. In a mixed point only the literal axis is group-relative: `(a.left.x, 60)` in a group at y=100 resolves to y=160. `kashika inspect file.kash <id>` prints a child's absolute box.
3. **Ids are global and bare.** Boxes, groups, images, junctions, lines and texts share one namespace, so a line can't be named after a box. Write `db.left`, never `vpc.db.left`. The segment keywords `h v x y hv vh vhv hvh curve` and `arrow` can't be ids.
4. **Prefer relationships to numbers.** `at api.right+(80,0) pin=left`, `-> hv db.top`, `-> y queue.left`, `junction j at stock.bottom+(0,50)`. They keep working when a box moves; literals don't. In a row or grid of mixed shapes chain on corners (`at a.tr+(80,0) pin=tl`): edge anchors such as `right` sit on a cloud's or parallelogram's outline, so the spacing drifts (`kashika ref recipes`).
5. **The last segment sets the arrow direction.** Enter a `left`/`right` side horizontally and a `top`/`bottom` side vertically. In `hv` and `vh` the last letter is how you arrive, so `vh` goes into `left`/`right` and `hv` into `top`/`bottom`: `a.right -> hv b.top`, `a.top -> vh b.left`, or for a longer detour `a.right -> x 360 -> y b.left -> b.left`. `check` doesn't flag a diagonal, since some diagrams want them; put `ortho` on lines meant to run at right angles (boxes-and-arrows architecture, flowcharts) so any diagonal is an error. For box-and-arrow diagrams, `diagram … ortho` sets it for every line; `ortho=false` opts one line out.
6. **Space labeled lines.** `check` flags a label that hits a box or an arrowhead, not one that merely has no margin. Leave the label's `kashika measure` width plus about 30px between the boxes it sits between, or move it with `label@t` or `label-side=above`.
7. **Text inside a shape goes in its text block.** A free `text` over a box is a `label-collision`; put it in the box's `{ }` block with `at (x,y)` in box-local coordinates (`kashika ref text`).
8. **`allow=` hides findings; it doesn't fix them.** Suppress narrowly and only once the layout settles: `allow=cross:l2 reason="non-planar"` silences crossings with `l2` only. Suppressed findings still count in the summary.
9. **`check` covers geometry only:** crossings, overlaps, near-misses, boxes in the way, crowded ports, contrast, labels on things, the canvas and the grid. It doesn't prove that arrows point the right way, that the right boxes are connected, or that the labels say what you mean.

## Syntax

```text
diagram "<title>" [size (w,h)] [margin=N] [kashika="0.1.0"] [grid=N] [clearance=N] [background=C] [icons="<dir>"] [allow=<code>,...] [ortho]
import "<file.kash>" [as <ns>]
style <name> { key=value ... }
shape <name> { path "..." } | shape <name> from "<file.svg>"
box   <id> ["<label>"] at <point> [size (w,h)] [pin=<anchor>] [shape=<shape>] [style=<name>] [layer=under|over] [sublabel="..."] [icon="<file>"] [style keys] [{ text block }]
group <id> ["<label>"] at <point> [size (w|auto, h|auto)] [pin=<anchor>] [style=<name>] [sublabel="..."] [title=top|bottom] { children }
junction <id> at <point> [dot]
text  [<id>] "<content>" at <point> [font-size=N] [color=C] [text-align=left|center|right] [muted]
image <id> "<file>" at <point> size (w,h) [pin=<anchor>] [fit=contain|cover|stretch]
line  [<id>] <point> -> <segment> [-> <segment> ...] [line attributes] [ortho]
arrow [<id>] <point> -> <segment> [-> <segment> ...] [line attributes] [ortho]   // a line with end=arrow
```

- `diagram` comes first, then any `import`s. Units are pixels. Without `size` the canvas fits everything drawn, plus `margin=N` (default 20) on the right and bottom; `size (w, auto)` or `size (auto, h)` fits one axis. A fitted canvas never grows left or up, so keep content at x, y ≥ 0. `margin=` with a fixed `size` is an error. The canvas is transparent unless `background=C`.
- One statement per line: a second statement keyword on the same line is a `parse` error. A line that starts with `->`, `key=` or `ortho` continues the statement above. The label is the quoted string after the id (there is no `label=` on a box). After the id and label, `at`, `size` and attributes may come in any order.
- Comments are `//` and `/* */`. `#` starts a color (`#rgb`, `#rrggbb`, `#rrggbbaa`, or a CSS name like `steelblue`), never a comment. Strings use `\"`, `\\` and `\n`. Only ASCII spaces, tabs and line breaks separate tokens; a non-breaking or zero-width space is a `parse` error.
- Style keys: `fill`, `stroke`, `stroke-width`, `stroke-style=solid|dashed|dotted`, `font-size`, `font-weight=normal|bold`, `color`, `radius`, `padding`, `muted`, `text-align`, `text-valign`, `title` (groups). Inline keys override `style=`, which overrides the built-in. Built-in styles: `service`, `datastore`, `external`, `queue`, `boundary`, `note` (`kashika ref styles`).
- `shape=` takes a built-in shape (`rect`, `rounded`, `cylinder`, `diamond`, `ellipse`, `hexagon`, `doc`, `cloud`, `person`, `parallelogram`, `predefined-process`, `stadium`, `triangle`, `octagon`, `note`, `package`, `queue`) or one you define or import (`kashika ref shapes`).
- A box without `size` fits its text (`kashika ref sizing`); a box with no text needs a `size`, and so does an image. A group without `size` (or with an `auto` axis) fits its children plus `padding`, and at least its title. Nothing inside a fitted group may reference the group itself (`placement-cycle`).
- `pin=<anchor>` puts that anchor of the new shape on `at` (default `tl`): `at api.right+(80,0) pin=left`. `side@t` works too: `pin=left@0.25`.
- Groups hold `box`, `group`, `junction`, `text` and `line`, use the `boundary` style, and reserve a title band at the top (at the bottom with `title=bottom`; a fitted group then adds it below its children). A junction is a named point lines can start or end at; it draws nothing without `dot`, so one without `dot` also works as a guide that other shapes are placed against (`kashika ref recipes`).
- `layer=under|over` on a box or image (not a group) paints it first or last. Layered shapes are backdrops and highlights, never obstacles: `overlap`, `through`, `near`, `label-collision`, `endpoint-off-edge`, `route` and `--corridors` ignore them; `spill`, `out-of-canvas` and their own text checks still apply. A `layer=under` shape inside a group paints right after that group's fill. Otherwise shapes paint in file order, then lines, then labels and texts.

```kash
diagram "Board" size (200, 120)
box a1 at (20, 20) size (80, 80) fill=#b58863 stroke=none layer=under
box b1 at (100, 20) size (80, 80) fill=#f0d9b5 stroke=none layer=under
box k "K" at (40, 40) size (40, 40)
box ring at (36, 36) size (48, 48) fill=none stroke=#dd3333 radius=24 layer=over
```

## Anchors and points

```text
point := ((x,y) | ref | (point)) [+(dx,dy) | -(dx,dy)]...
ref   := <id>.<anchor> | <junction-id>
x, y  := number | ref.x | ref.y        // mixed point: (db.left.x, lane.y)
```

| anchor | position |
|---|---|
| `top` `bottom` `left` `right` | edge midpoints |
| `top@t` `bottom@t` | along the edge from left to right, `0 <= t <= 1` |
| `left@t` `right@t` | along the edge from top to bottom |
| `center` | center |
| `tl` `tr` `bl` `br` | bounding-box corners; also `top-left` and so on |

- `+(dx,dy)` adds an offset and `-(dx,dy)` subtracts it; parts are signed either way, so `db.top+(-10,0)` is `db.top-(10,0)`. Offsets combine with `@t`: `db.left@0.25-(0,4)`.
- Offsets may follow any point and add up: `(10,20)+(5,0)`, `(db.left.x, lane.y)+(0,26)`, `db.top+(0,4)+(10,0)`.
- Parentheses around a point change nothing and must be closed: `(db.center+(0,40))` is `db.center+(0,40)`, and `(db.center)+(0,40)` is the same point. They nest at most 16 deep: `((db.left.x, lane.y)+(0,4))`.
- `.x`/`.y` only work inside a parenthesized mixed point. The `x`/`y` segments take a whole point: `x bi.bottom`, not `x bi.bottom.x`.
- Groups have anchors too: `vpc.top`.
- Edge anchors on non-rectangular shapes move straight onto the outline, so a vertical line into `db.top` stays vertical.

## Line paths

A line is `start -> segment -> segment ...`. Each segment ends at a point, which starts the next one. The optional id follows `line` directly, with no colon: `line api_db api.right -> db.left`. `arrow` is the same statement with `end=arrow` as its default: `arrow api_db api.right -> db.left`; an explicit `end=` wins. `arrow` can't be an id.

| segment | path |
|---|---|
| `<point>` | straight to the point |
| `h dx` / `v dy` | move by dx / dy; negative goes left / up |
| `x X` / `y Y` | move along one axis to x=X / y=Y; X or Y is a number or a point (`y db.top`, not `y db.top.y`). It stops level with the point, not on it: end with the anchor (`-> y db.left -> db.left`) or use `vh db.left` |
| `hv <point>` | horizontal, then vertical: one elbow |
| `vh <point>` | vertical, then horizontal |
| `vhv <point>` / `hvh <point>` | vertical, horizontal halfway, vertical (or the other way round): two elbows; `vhv@t` puts the middle run `t` of the way (`0 < t < 1`); straight when the point is level |
| `curve left N <point>` | curve that bulges N px to the left of the direction of travel |
| `curve right N <point>` | the same, to the right |
| `curve via (c1) (c2) <point>` | cubic Bézier with explicit control points |

| attribute | default | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `start=` `end=` | `none` (`end` on `arrow`: `arrow`) | `none`, `arrow`, `open`, `triangle`, `diamond`, `hollow-diamond`, `dot`, `circle`, `one`, `many`, `one-many`, `zero-one`, `zero-many`. `arrow`, `open`, `diamond` and `dot` put their tip on the endpoint; the hollow UML ends `triangle` (generalization), `hollow-diamond` (aggregation) and `circle` show the fill beneath. Ends grow with `stroke-width` above 1.5. Crow's-foot (ER) ends `one`, `many`, `one-many`, `zero-one`, `zero-many` sit on the line without trimming it; a `zero-*` circle is open |
| `label="..."` `label@t="..."` | `t=0.5` | label at fraction `t` of the path length, markup allowed; repeatable |
| `label-side=` | `on` | `on` centers labels on the line and cuts it out under them; `above`/`below` (horizontal segment) or `left`/`right` (vertical) put them beside it, 4px off the stroke, unmasked |
| `radius=N` | `0` | rounds every corner |
| `stroke` `stroke-width` `stroke-style` `style=` | style | a style applies only its stroke and font keys |
| `ortho` | off (on under `diagram … ortho`) | any diagonal or curved segment is an error; `ortho=false` opts a line out of the diagram's `ortho` |
| `allow=<code>[:<id>],...` `reason="..."` | | suppress findings |

**The last segment sets the arrow direction.** Enter `left`/`right` with a horizontal hop (`vh`, `hvh`, or `x`/`y` and then the anchor) and `top`/`bottom` with a vertical one (`hv`, `vhv`). Name your lines; unnamed ones are `line@L<n>`.

**Labels beside the line.** `label-side` applies to every label of the line. A side that doesn't match the segment under the label (`above` on a vertical segment, anything but `on` on a diagonal or curve) is an `attr` error, and the label stays on the line. At a corner either segment counts.

```kash
diagram "Messages" size (320, 140) kashika="0.1.0"
arrow req (20, 40) -> h 280 label="request" label-side=above
arrow res (300, 100) -> h -280 stroke-style=dashed label="reply" label-side=below
```

## Fixing diagnostics

Errors stop `render` (unless `--force`) and are listed first; fix them before warnings. `kashika ref lints` lists every code. The fixes needed most:

- `parse`: fix these first. A statement that fails to parse is dropped, but references to its id are not reported again as `unknown-id`.
- `label-overflow`: use the size in the help, shorten the text, break it with `\n`, lower `padding=` on a small box, or leave `size` out.
- `cross` / `through` / `near`: move the segment to a lane from the `free` help, or ask `kashika route`. `allow=cross:<other>` only for a non-planar graph. `through` or `near` on a line's own endpoint box means the elbow is the wrong way round: enter a `left`/`right` side with `vh` or an `x …` hop, and a `top`/`bottom` side with `hv` or a `y …` hop.
- `shared-segment` / `port-crowding`: spread the ports with `side@t` (`core.right@0.3`, `core.right@0.7`), or fan out from a `junction`.
- `not-ortho` / `diagonal-tail`: finish with `hv`/`vh` or `x`/`y`.
- `label-collision`: move a line label to a `label@t` from the help, widen the gap to the run the help names, or use `label-side`. Route lines below a group's title band, or move the title with `text-align=right` or `title=bottom` on the group. A free `text` over a box goes into the box's block.
- `near-axis` / `jog`: a slant or a small step between runs a few px apart; use the level anchor from the help (`cdn.left@0.42`), or `hv`/`vh`.
- `out-of-canvas`: leave `size` off the `diagram` line so the canvas fits, and keep content at x, y ≥ 0.
- `missing-glyph`: replace CJK and similar characters with an `icon=` or `image`; emoji and box drawing are covered.
- `unused-allow`: the `allow=` entry no longer suppresses anything; remove it, and `reason=` if no entry is left.
- `text-conflict`: move `label`/`sublabel` into the block as its first items, or give every block item an `at`.

## Topics

`kashika ref <topic>` prints one of these; `kashika ref <code>` explains a diagnostic.

| topic | covers |
|---|---|
| `rules` | the rules people get wrong |
| `syntax` | every statement and its clauses, groups, junctions and layers |
| `anchors` | anchors, `@t`, offsets and mixed points |
| `paths` | segments, line ends, labels and `ortho` |
| `sizing` | fitted boxes, and how big each shape must be for its text |
| `text` | markup, sublabels, alignment and text blocks |
| `shapes` | built-in and custom shapes, SVG shapes, images, icons and imports |
| `styles` | style keys, colors and the built-in styles |
| `recipes` | routing patterns, fan-out, self-loops, sequence diagrams, ER tables, guides and graphs |
| `examples` | a complete worked diagram |
| `lints` | every diagnostic code with its trigger and fix, and how to suppress one |
| `metrics` | font metrics and average character widths |
| `cli` | every command and flag: check, render, inspect, route, measure, fmt, icons and ref |
| `limits` | raster and file limits, and what is not supported |
